//! Repro: a PLAIN STRUCT with an `Option(Box(Self))` field + `derive(Clone)`.
//!
//!   TS      -> rc=0, prints "box-self-struct cloned"
//!   yo-self -> C compile fails (rc=1)
//!
//! This is a SMALLER and STRICTLY BETTER reproducer than
//! `box-self-recursive-enum-derive-clone.yo` — read that file for the full
//! investigation record; this one isolates the variable that actually matters.
//!
//! ISOLATION MATRIX (all three run against the SAME s1 binary, so the only
//! difference is the source shape):
//!
//!   struct(inner : Box(i32))            + derive(Clone) ..... WORKS (rc=0)
//!   struct(inner : Box(Inner))          + derive(Clone) ..... WORKS (rc=0)
//!     (Inner is an ordinary struct that also derives Clone)
//!   struct(next  : Option(Box(Self)))   + derive(Clone) ..... FAILS
//!
//! So it is NOT "Box over a non-primitive" and NOT enum-specific and NOT
//! about recursing through enum VARIANTS — the recursive enum in the older
//! repro was incidental. The single operative ingredient is `Self` inside the
//! boxed field type.
//!
//! It also spans TWO of the handoff's three red families at once, which makes
//! it the cheapest way in:
//!
//!   error: argument may not have 'void' type            <- "missing ctor"
//!   error: too few arguments to function call, 'self'
//!   error: assigning to '__yo_t15' from incompatible
//!          type '__yo_t1'                               <- "struct-instance
//!                                                           identity"
//!
//! MEASURED CAUSAL CHAIN (probes against the running compiler; see the older
//! repro for the raw traces):
//!
//!   1. The generic-impl MATCH is innocent. Probing the real
//!      `find_methods_from_generic_impls` (evaluator/values/impl.yo:880 — NOT
//!      the dead duplicate in generic_impl_registry.yo, which nothing
//!      imports) prints `binds=<the finalized type>`, i.e. the correct
//!      `T := <Self's concrete type>`, and exactly ONE candidate. So
//!      property_access.yo:845 takes its `len() == 1` path and installs the
//!      capture-injected `method_value`.
//!   2. Captures ARE bound into the body-eval env (function.yo:3349).
//!   3. And yet `Box.clone`'s body is evaluated GENERICALLY: inside it
//!      `self.*` has type `V`, so the nested `box(self.*.clone())` sees an
//!      arg of type `V` and the name-match arm (function.yo:1168, which has
//!      no `is_some_type` guard) binds `V := V`. That is the emitted
//!      `yo_id_2747_V_id_1598_…(void value)` — a specialization over an
//!      unresolved forall, constructing the generic `Box(V)`.
//!
//! So: the correct binding is produced AND installed, and is then lost before
//! the body runs. The defect is that `self` is typed `Box(V)` instead of
//! `Box(<concrete>)` when that body is evaluated — one level ABOVE the `box`
//! call where the symptom surfaces.
//!
//! EIGHT HYPOTHESES ARE NOW DISPROVEN — do not retry any of them:
//!   1. derive-specific (a hand-written impl fails identically)
//!   2. return-type inference (an explicit `-> Self` fails identically)
//!   3. missing `Acyclic`
//!   4. forward-ref captures (`_build_forward_ref_funcval`)
//!   5. receiver `Self` (a fully concrete receiver still fails)
//!   6. recursion guards (probed: ZERO hits, never fire here)
//!   7. self-shell leaking into the forall binding. The leak is REAL — a
//!      shell nested in a generic's `type_arguments` is never resolved,
//!      because every `resolve_enum_shell`/`resolve_struct_shell` call is
//!      top-level only — but resolving it at both forall-binding arms of
//!      `_funcval_bind_foralls` produced BYTE-IDENTICAL output. Reverted.
//!      It could not have worked: the bogus spec binds `V` to its OWN SomeT,
//!      not to the shell.
//!   8. refusing the bind-to-self (`V := V`) in the name-match arm. Made it
//!      STRICTLY WORSE: 2 -> 4 `void`-type errors, plus a new `field has
//!      incomplete type 'void'`, and the generic ctor became DECLARED as
//!      `__yo_new___yo_t13(void _u42_)`. Leaving the forall unbound just
//!      propagates the unresolved param into the struct definition. Reverted.
//!      Lesson: the binding arm is the SYMPTOM site, not the defect site —
//!      fixing it there cannot help, because by then the arg type is already
//!      generic.
//!
//! READING THE SPEC NAME (needed to interpret any future probe). The segment
//! after the fid is `<bound-type-name>_id_<bound-type-id>`, NOT
//! `<forall-name>_id_…` — the CORRECT sibling spec in the same emit renders
//! `yo_id_2747_TreeNode_id_enum_decl_56319…`. So `yo_id_2747_V_id_1598` is
//! `box`'s forall `V` bound to a SomeType that is itself named `V` — i.e.
//! `box`'s OWN generic original being specialized against itself. The two
//! foralls in play are distinct and must not be conflated:
//!   `box  :: fn(forall(V : Type), own(value) : V) -> Box(V)`  (prelude:7368)
//!   `impl(forall(T : Type), where(T <: Clone), Box(T), Clone(...))` (:7395)
//! Note `Box` is NOT a generic struct — it is a comptime FUNCTION
//! `fn(comptime(V) : Type) -> comptime(Type)` (prelude:7360).
//!
//! That the #8 guard did NOT remove this spec, despite it being a
//! self-binding, says the generic original is emitted through a path that
//! does not go via the name-match arm. Find that path before guarding
//! anything else.
//!
//!   9. Stale FRAME LEVELS in the method-type substitution. `substitute`
//!      replaces a SomeT only when `subst_lookup(s, name, level)` matches on
//!      BOTH name and `frame_level` (types/substitution.yo:100), and
//!      `find_methods_from_generic_impls` keys `spec_s` from
//!      `entry.forall_some_types[i]` while the `T` inside the stored method
//!      type was baked at impl-DEFINITION time — so a level mismatch would
//!      silently leave `Box(T)` unsubstituted. Tested by adding a name-only
//!      substitution key (`subst_new_name_only`) plus a probe that fires
//!      whenever a name-only lookup succeeds where the exact key would have
//!      missed. Result: ZERO probe hits, repro unchanged. The exact key never
//!      misses; `substitute` is NOT no-opping. REVERTED (pure no-op).
//!
//! WHERE THAT LEAVES IT. The bogus emission is a SPECIALIZATION, not a bare
//! generic original — `_is_generic_unspecialized_func`
//! (codegen/functions/collection.yo:287) already skips anything whose type
//! still has `forall_labels`, and the spec has none, so it passes collection
//! legitimately. Per an earlier probe it is created at the
//! `create_specialized_function_inline` call site
//! (evaluator/calls/function.yo:1623) while `ctx.currently_specializing_function`
//! is the OUTER `clone` spec. So the defect is upstream of codegen entirely:
//! something evaluates `Box.clone`'s body with its forall unresolved and mints
//! a specialization from it.
//!
//! Since hypothesis 9 shows `method_type` IS substituted correctly, the
//! remaining suspect is that the BODY evaluation does not use it: check
//! whether the FuncVal body is typed from `fv_param_types` (which comes from
//! the callee's `ExprInfo.ty`, i.e. the substituted `method_type` —
//! function.yo:2899) or is re-derived from the FuncVal's own declared param
//! type expressions. PROBE THAT DIRECTLY — print `self`'s bound type at the
//! top of `Box.clone`'s body evaluation — rather than reasoning about it; the
//! last three hypotheses all looked airtight on paper and were all wrong.
//!
//! PROBE 3 (INCONCLUSIVE — recorded so it is redesigned, not repeated).
//! Printed `self`'s bound declared type at the FuncVal param-binding site
//! (function.yo:3467, `bind_decl_pt`), filtered to types whose
//! `type_to_string` contains "Box". ZERO hits on this repro.
//!
//! Do NOT conclude "Box.clone binds self on a different path" from that. The
//! FILTER is the weak link: `type_to_string` renders some struct types as an
//! unnamed `<struct:struct_yo_id_N>` (visible in the PROBEFM output in the
//! sibling repro, where non-Box patterns printed exactly that way), so a
//! `Box(...)` param could render without the substring and be filtered out.
//! The probe cannot distinguish "never bound here" from "bound here but
//! rendered unnamed". REDESIGN IT: print EVERY `self` binding unfiltered
//! (optionally with the func_id) and grep afterwards.
//!
//! Two facts to carry into that redesign, both read from source:
//!   * `create_specialized_function_inline` (helper.yo:1182-2020) never calls
//!     `add_variable_to_env` for `cap_names`/`cap_tys`/`cap_vals` — it only
//!     COPIES them into a new FuncVal. It binds PARAMS into the caller-supplied
//!     `callee_env`.
//!   * That `callee_env` is `fresh_env`, which IS the env whose captures were
//!     bound at function.yo:3349 (only one `fresh_env` is constructed in that
//!     file, at :3319, and it is threaded into the extracted runtime-call
//!     helper that makes the :1623 spec call). So the impl's `T` capture
//!     SHOULD be in scope for the spec body — which is why the "captures never
//!     reach the body" story is NOT yet established, and why the unfiltered
//!     probe is needed before any fix.
//!
//! NEXT STEP: find why `Box.clone`'s body is evaluated with `self : Box(V)`
//! when the candidate's `method_type` was already substituted to the concrete
//! receiver (`substitute(spec_s, ftype)`, impl.yo:~990). Concretely: determine
//! whether the call path types the parameters from that substituted
//! `method_type` or re-derives them from the FuncVal's own declared param
//! types — and if the latter, whether the impl-forall captures are in scope at
//! that moment. That is a STATIC question about the call path; answer it by
//! reading before spending another ~40-minute s1 build on a probe.
open(import("std/fmt"));

Node :: struct(v : i32, next : Option(Box(Self)));
derive(Node, Clone);

main :: (fn() -> unit)({
  n := Node(v : i32(1), next : Option(Box(Node)).None);
  n2 := n.clone();
  println(`box-self-struct cloned`);
});

export(main);
